<body>
    <main class="container">

        <h1>Opening Remarks</h1>

        <div class="subtitle">
            Religious Education Training
        </div>

        <p>
            Brothers and sisters, good morning, and welcome. Thank you for
            giving up your Saturday morning. You teach our youth and young
            adults week after week, often with little recognition, and the
            Lord sees every bit of it.
        </p>

        <p>
            I want to begin with why we are here. The purpose of religious
            education is to help each student become a lifelong disciple of
            Jesus Christ. Elder Nelson once taught that we should begin with
            the end in mind. If we keep that goal in front of us, everything
            else follows: what we teach, how we teach, and even how we measure
            whether it worked.
        </p>

        <p>
            How we teach matters. Elder Meredith reminded us of President
            Packer’s cake. It was the same cake in both cases, but the way it
            was served decided whether anyone wanted it. The gospel is the
            same, but our manner of teaching can make it inviting or make it
            hard to receive.
        </p>

        <p>
            Today’s training is built on one idea from
            <em>Teaching in the Savior’s Way</em>: invite diligent learning.
            There are three parts.
        </p>

        <section class="teaching-point">
            <h2>1. Invite Learners to Prepare to Learn</h2>

            <p>
                Even the brother of Jared was first invited by the Lord to
                take responsibility for the solution. The Lord asked him what
                he wanted prepared before he acted. Our students are the same.
                When they prepare, the discussion is deeper and the Spirit is
                stronger.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="teaching-point">
            <h2>2. Encourage Learners to Share What They Are Learning</h2>

            <p>
                When students know they will be asked to share, they listen
                and prepare differently. Speaking what they have learned also
                makes it clearer to them.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="teaching-point">
            <h2>3. Invite Learners to Live What They Are Learning</h2>

            <p>
                It was awe-inspiring to watch the Savior walk on water, but
                watching was not enough for Peter. He needed to have the
                experience himself. Our students are the same. Faith grows
                when it is acted on.
            </p>
        </section>

        <p>
            As we listen to Sister Teboho Follie today, I invite you to do two
            things. First, see your students as God sees them, capable of
            learning and acting for themselves. Second, ask yourself honestly
            where you could invite more of that learning in your own class.
            Then listen for the Spirit to show you how.
        </p>

        <p>
            We are not only passing on information, and we are not only
            inspiring students with our personalities. We are preparing them
            to recognize the voice of the Holy Ghost. That skill will bless
            them long after they leave our classrooms.
        </p>

        <div class="testimony">
            <p>
                I testify that the Lord knows you and magnifies your efforts.
                As you invite your students to act, you will see miracles in
                their lives. I say this in the name of Jesus Christ, amen.
            </p>
        </div>

    </main>
